	url = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11469787/},
	doi = {10.1109/ICVADV67766.2026.11469787},
	abstract = {Accurate estimation of critical lithium-ion battery states remains a significant challenge for Battery Management Systems (BMS) in electric vehicles. This paper presents a framework that integrates a data-driven Digital Twin with adaptive machine learning to improve the estimation of State of Charge (SoC), State of Health (SoH), and Remaining Useful Life (RUL). The methodology evaluates six machine learning algorithms on a dataset of over 1,150 real-world battery operation samples using 5-fold cross-validation. A neural network model demonstrated the best performance with an R≤ of 0.7789. Comparative analysis with literature suggests this performance is competitive with traditional Extended Kalman Filter approaches. The integrated system includes an Isolation Forest-based anomaly detection module with 92\% precision and a continuous adaptive learning mechanism that yielded a 13.71\% improvement in accuracy. Experimental validation on operational data indicates system robustness under varied conditions, suggesting a pathway toward more adaptive and predictive battery management.},
