	doi = {10.1109/INFOTEH68759.2026.11477647},
	abstract = {The convergence of Building Information Modeling (BIM) and Internet of Things (IoT) is critical for the development of Digital Twins in smart buildings. However, a signiﬁcant gap remains between the static, structured nature of BIM data and the dynamic, unstructured nature of high-frequency IoT telemetry. This paper presents the “WiseBuild” architecture deployed at the CeDInt research centre, which bridges this gap using an Ontology-Based Data Access (OBDA) approach. We detail the deployment of a comprehensive sensor network consisting of 29 BatMeter energy analyzers and environmental sensors integrated into a ThingsBoard infrastructure. Using Ontop Command Line Interface (CLI) and a custom domain ontology aligned with Building Topology Ontology (BOT) and Sensor, Observation, Sample, and Actuator (SOSA), we expose a Virtual Knowledge Graph (VKG) that enables semantic querying of Structured Query Language (SQL)-based time-series data. We validate this architecture through a practical use case: an endto-end Energy Forecasting Toolkit that consumes semantically enriched data to train Deep Learning models such as Long Short Term Memory models (LSTM) and Transformers, without requiring hard-coded sensor identiﬁers.},
