	doi = {10.1109/ICIEAM69213.2026.11549652},
	abstract = {This paper presents an intelligent phytolighting system based on Internet of Things (IoT) technologies and a hybrid machine learning model for optimizing plant growth in controlled environments. The proposed system integrates multispectral LED lighting, distributed environmental sensors, and a closed-loop control algorithm combining LSTM-based growth prediction with gradient-based optimization of spectral parameters. A dynamic spectral control strategy is developed to adapt light intensity and wavelength composition according to plant growth stages and environmental conditions. The system architecture includes sensor, edge, and cloud layers enabling real-time data acquisition and predictive control. Experimental results obtained in a greenhouse study demonstrate a 28\% increase in biomass yield and a 20\% reduction in energy consumption compared to conventional static lighting systems. The results confirm that adaptive, data-driven phytolighting significantly improves both productivity and energy efficiency in controlled-environment agriculture. Furthermore, the proposed approach enables continuous real-time adaptation to environmental disturbances and plant physiological responses. The developed framework can serve as a foundation for fully autonomous smart greenhouse systems and digital twin applications in agriculture.},
