	abstract = {The paper describes an end-to-end system which integrates physical simulation and machine learning to predict inevitabilities within wind turbines before they happen. A Python based simulation platform represents the entire turbine system aerodynamics, mechanical subsystems and electrical elements creating synthetic operational data that encompasses normal operating conditions and several fault scenarios. This is the direct solution to one of the major wind turbine fault prediction challenges: the lack of actual fault data. By generating highvolume, balanced, and labelled training data, the framework facilitates building of quality machine learning models that would otherwise prove extremely hard to develop on actual turbine operations. A framework classifier trained on this synthetic dataset achieves high accuracy in the classification of various types of faults. The framework demonstrates a total end-to-end pipeline physics driven data generation verified through successful machine learning deployment compared to actual operational data of an NREL 5-MW reference wind turbine. The Random Forest model offers interpretable fault predictions and extensive featureimportance extraction, enabling operators to discover which sensor variables are most indicative of certain fault types, such as bearing deterioration, gearbox failures, generator failures, and structural blade damage.},
