	abstract = {Semiconductor demand has expanded fabrication (FAB) facilities and their energy use, with heating, ventilation, and air-conditioning (HVAC) systems forming a dominant load. Improving HVAC ef1ciency is crucial for cost, reliability, and decarbonization. This study aims to reduce HVAC energy use in an operating FAB by coupling a high-1delity digital twin with arti1cial intelligence (AI)-driven predictive control. We develop a calibrated digital twin using the EnergyPlus simulation engine and Isight; automated calibration integrates Optimal Latin Hypercube Sampling in a design-of-experiments stage and a pointer-based data-matching routine to converge parameters. The model reproduces measured energy with strong agreement (R2 = 0.98; CVRMSE = 1.22 \%), comfortably satisfying ASHRAE Guideline 14 limits. Real-time prediction is implemented through MATLAB–Building Controls Virtual Test Bed integration, where a deep neural network forecasts HVAC energy 1 h ahead (R2 = 0.99). Using these forecasts, a dynamic control algorithm proactively adjusts cooling-water temperature and @ow to minimize energy use. Relative to conventional static control, the strategy yields approximately 9.4 \% energy savings without degrading operation. These results show that a calibrated digital twin combined with AI prediction can deliver measurable, real-time ef1ciency gains in semiconductor HVAC systems. The key contribution is an end-to-end, reproducible work@ow—from automated calibration to deployment-ready predictive control—that shortens calibration effort and operationalizes datadriven optimization in a live FAB. This study demonstrates a digital twin–AI framework that achieves veri1ed energy savings in a semiconductor FAB HVAC system, providing a meaningful case study and a practical template for scale-out and future integration with multi-objective supervisory control.},
	language = {en},
