	url = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11566636/},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCRTEE68719.2026.11566636},
	abstract = {Interruptions from animals and birds continue to be a significant safety and operations problem in railroad tracks, especially in the rural and forested sites. Through the use of an AI-based digital twin paradigm, this paper proposes a realistic intrusion prevention solution by adaptive deterrent, risk prediction and early detection, where no continuous physical barriers will exist. The framework features intelligent categorization, multi-source sensing and a virtual railway corridor which are synchronized to predict incursion incidents and initiate the targeted deterrent prior to the arrival of the train. Performance was evaluated through real-time sensor and train operational data, simulation and pilot deployment. The results were found to be accurate for the prediction lead time of nearly 5 minutes and end-to-end response time of less than 1 second for the detection of wildlife, cattle and low flying birds with the system. While reducing needless activation and ecological disruption, adaptive deterrence dramatically decreased intrusion events. The results show that digitally coordinated, predictive action can contribute to the safety of the railway corridor, its scalability and environmental sustainability.},
	language = {en},
