	url = {https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3808707.3808820},
	doi = {10.1145/3808707.3808820},
	abstract = {With the rapid advancement of urbanization and growing environmental concerns, real-time landscape performance monitoring has become crucial for sustainable urban development. This paper proposes a novel deep learning-based digital twin framework that enables real-time simulation and optimization of landscape performance indicators. The framework integrates a hierarchical architecture consisting of physical layer for multi-sensor data acquisition, data layer for preprocessing and fusion, model layer employing hybrid CNN-LSTM neural networks, and application layer for visualization and decision support. The proposed hybrid deep learning model eﬀectively captures both spatial patterns through convolutional operations and temporal dependencies through recurrent mechanisms, achieving accurate prediction of key environmental parameters including temperature distribution, humidity variation, air quality, noise levels, and light intensity. Knowledge distillation and model quantization techniques are employed to reduce computational overhead while maintaining prediction accuracy, enabling deployment on resource-constrained edge devices. Extensive experiments conducted on a 6-month dataset from a 15-hectare urban park with 42 sensor nodes demonstrate that our method achieves superior performance with RMSE of 0.428°C for temperature prediction, representing 63.2\% improvement over CFD-based methods and 28.3\% improvement over standard LSTM approaches. The system maintains real-time performance with end-to-end latency of 8 seconds and achieves 𝑅2 scores exceeding 0.92 across all performance indicators, validating its eﬀectiveness for intelligent landscape management and sustainable design optimization.},
	language = {en},
