	url = {https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S1359431126010227},
	doi = {10.1016/j.applthermaleng.2026.130714},
	abstract = {Heat pumps are a key technology for decarbonising the building sector and for supporting the electricity grid through the provision of energy .exibility services. Regarding the latter aspect, accurate heat pump control is essential. However, most available models operate at time steps above )fteen minutes and do not reach the energy .exibility targets set by the energy management system. Achieving high control accuracy is challenging due to internal control strategies set by manufacturers to ensure safety and reliability, which can generally not be bypassed and signi)cantly affect operation. Developing a digital twin, capable of incorporating these internal strategies is therefore crucial. Leveraging the proven capabilities of machine learning to uncover complex relationships with minimal human intervention, this work develops a digital twin of a water/water heat pump using black-box models. Its main novelty lies in combining space heating and domestic hot water services prediction in a single model. Experimental data from a hardware-in-the-loop setup at one-minute intervals is used. Several modelling approaches are compared, all based on long short-term memory neural networks with different architectures. Performance is evaluated through prediction simulations, including one-step-ahead forecasts which predict the immediate next value and multi-step-ahead forecasts which extend further into the future. Results show that the best performance is obtained by combining two submodels. The )rst submodel predicts the mode of operation, while the second submodel forecasts temperatures, .ow rates and power. This approach achieved multi-step-ahead prediction accuracies of 54.91\% within a ± 10\% error for electrical power, 85.34\% within a ± 1\% error for the condenser outlet temperature and 71.58\% within a ± 10\% error for the condenser pump .ow rate.},
	language = {en},
