	url = {http://digital-library.theiet.org/doi/10.1049/icp.2024.4387},
	doi = {10.1049/icp.2024.4387},
	abstract = {This paper investigates the application of Digital Twin technology and machine learning in high-voltage transmission systems, aiming to overcome the challenges associated with real-time monitoring and fault prediction in traditional systems. It introduces the definition, core principles, and application architecture of Digital Twin technology in the context of high-voltage transmission, and provides a detailed account of the design and implementation of a digital twin model. The process includes two key steps: physical modeling and data fusion. By leveraging data analysis and machine learning, fault prediction models are constructed to enhance prediction accuracy and system stability. A case study illustrates the practical application and potential challenges of these methods in real-world high-voltage transmission systems, with particular focus on data quality, model generalization, and real-time performance. Experimental results demonstrate that integrating Digital Twin technology with machine learning can significantly improve fault prediction, minimize unexpected outages, and boost the overall reliability and safety of transmission systems.},
