	doi = {10.1109/EI268505.2025.11425425},
	abstract = {The integration of large-scale wind power into the Energy Internet presents significant challenges for grid security due to sudden turbine component failures. This study presents a sequential LSTM–Transformer framework for early fault prediction of wind turbine gearbox and bearing components, utilizing high-frequency sensor data from the NREL dataset. The model captures both short-term temporal dynamics and longrange dependencies in multivariate vibration and temperature signals, enabling accurate, pre-fault predictions. Based on these forecasts, a predict-and-derate control strategy is developed to turn abrupt turbine shutdowns into managed, grid-friendly power adjustments. System-level simulations show that this approach smooths plant-level power output, reducing the Rate of Change of Frequency by 25\% and lowering frequency nadir deviations by 18\% during impending failures. By connecting component-level predictive maintenance with system-level control, the framework provides a validated pathway for enhancing the security, resilience, and flexibility of wind-integrated power systems, highlighting the potential of predictive digital twin-enabled wind farm operation.},
