	abstract = {Background: In modern industrial performance, the energy-efficient and safe control of Electrical Automation Systems (EAS’s) is vital for sustainable operations under dynamically changing load conditions. Challenge: The existing Fussy Proportional Integral Derivative (PID) algorithm-based control model still struggle to maintain optimal tuning across multi-objective trade-offs between response speed, energy efficiency and stability. Proposed Model: To address these challenges, this research proposed an Energy-Adaptive Reinforcement Learning integrated with Safe Model Predictive Control (EARL-SMPC) framework for frequency conversion EAS respectively. Initially, the system integrates real-world laboratory data collected from a Programmable Logic Controller (PLC) and Variable Frequency Drive (VFD) motor setup to construct a digital twin for dynamic simulation as well as controller training. After that, RL agent learns optimal frequency modulation policies that minimize power consumption while maintaining speed precision. Finally, the SMPC layer operates RL outputs that filter and optimizes generated control actions to ensure constraint satisfaction and predictive stability. Results: Experimental analysis demonstrates that the EARL-SMPC framework achieves improved energy efficiency, stable speed–frequency response in term of reduced error of 0.04\%, under variable loads, outperforming existing Fuzzy PID-based control model.},
