	url = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11379040/},
	doi = {10.1109/ICCSIE67394.2025.11379040},
	abstract = {The autonomous navigation of power-substation inspection robots faces complex environmental challenges such as strong electromagnetic interference and dynamic obstacles. Traditional algorithms like A and RRT suffer from insufficient robustness and high replanning time consumption. This paper proposes a multimodal fusion - deep reinforcement learning collaborative autonomous navigation framework called MFDRL-Nav. The framework takes 3D LiDAR point clouds, visible light images, and IMU signals as inputs to construct a spatiotemporally aligned cross-modal observation tensor. It designs a hybrid observation encoder to map heterogeneous information into the same latent space and employs Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) to train a hierarchical policy network, with the upper layer outputting global subgoals and the lower layer outputting local velocity commands. The framework introduces curriculum-based simulation-to-reality transfer and a dynamic obstacle attention mechanism (DOA), accumulating 280 million steps of interaction data in a 1:1 highfidelity digital twin environment of a substation. A 180-day field test at five 500 kV substations shows that MF-DRL-Nav reduces the average path length by 19.7\%, has a replanning delay of less than 120 ms, and a collision rate of 0.3\%, significantly outperforming traditional methods and existing learning-based baselines, thus laying the foundation for the "unmanned operation" and "one-click sequence control" of substations.},
