	doi = {10.1109/ICCSIE67394.2025.11379291},
	abstract = {With the integration of distributed generation (DG), electric vehicles (EVs), and smart loads, modern distribution networks (DNs) exhibit high complexity and dynamic uncertainty, posing significant challenges to traditional reliability assessment (RA) methods. This paper proposes a hybrid RA framework fusing Digital Twin (DT) and Machine Learning (ML) to address the trade-off between assessment accuracy and computational efficiency. First, a high-fidelity DT model of the DN is constructed to realize real-time mapping of physical system states (e.g., load fluctuations, DG output, and equipment health) via 5G-edge computing. Second, a long short-term memory-gated recurrent unit (LSTM-GRU) hybrid model is designed to predict key fault parameters (fault duration, affected range) by leveraging historical fault data and real-time DT feedback. Third, an improved Monte Carlo Simulation (MCS) algorithm is proposed to prune low-probability fault scenarios based on DT-realized system states, reducing computational redundancy while ensuring assessment precision. The framework is validated on IEEE 33-bus and 123-bus test systems, with results showing that compared to traditional MCS and single ML methods, the proposed approach reduces SAIDI (System Average Interruption Duration Index) and SAIFI (System Average Interruption Frequency Index) prediction errors by 15.2\%-20.3\% and shortens computational time by 32.6\%-41.8\%. This work provides a practical tool for DN operators to optimize maintenance strategies and enhance power supply reliability.},
