	doi = {10.1109/TEMSCON-ASPAC65329.2025.11512271},
	abstract = {The Digital Twin (DT) technology represents a significant advancement in operational management of physical systems. It provides a virtual emulation that reflects a system’s status in real time. One domain where DT demonstrates particular value is in Energy Storage Systems (ESS). DT supports ESS by monitoring performance, lowering maintenance costs, and ensuring efficient operation of energy storage systems. This study focuses on designing a new methodology for implementing a small-scale fault detection DT from the ground up. The methodology is low-cost and adaptable, making it applicable for various processes. Some of the key technologies used include a microcontroller for data processing, sensors such as a thermocouple, a voltage sensor, and a current sensor for data collection, a machine learning model for fault identification, and an AutoCAD-based geometrical user interface. The methodology was applied to an ESS for verification, where the designed DT in this paper has successfully identified commonly occurring failure modes such as air leaks and overload conditions. These results suggest that an adaptive and affordable DT framework can be implemented for predictive maintenance, particularly in compact energy storage setups and similar applications.},
