	abstract = {The Proposed AI framework can perform things like improve printed circuit boards (PCBs), build up systemlevel integration, and keep a watch on production in real time. This framework will help you create electronics that operate well and are correct. To attain these goals, we will: There are a variety of different technologies employed in this pipeline, such as cross-layer digital twins, probabilistic error modeling, machine vision, and reinforcement learning. Because of this, it may (i) improve both the soldering and motion paths at the same time, (ii) discover and fix placement issues, and (iii) pass on decisions made earlier to throughput, cost, and reliability goals for modules. When there are a lot of boards and lines nearby, the method works incredibly well. The system gets a 95.2\% for its equipment, a 91.8\% for its predictive maintenance, an 89.5\% for optimizing energy, and a 93.2\% for making the most of its resources. It also finds faults and mistakes 96.5\% of the time and decreases the time it takes to complete a task by 87.4\%. The approach performs a good job of addressing all of these requirements. After these changes were made, the Cpk/Ppk ratio rose up from 1.18/1.10 to 1.72/1.63. This makes the ratio a lot better. The first-pass yield is 96.9\% (+5.0 pp), the DPMO is 45.4\% lower, the AOI false alarm rate is 60.4\% lower, and the verified escape rate is 57.1\% lower. We noticed that all of these things happened the same way. All of these successes are due in large part to the introduction of a higher quality standard. Our results suggest that a hybrid technique that combines learning and control could be able to achieve the best accuracy, latency, and energy profiles for production and be more resistant to threshold choice than the standard method of adjusting each station independently.},
