	doi = {10.1109/CESPE68033.2025.00091},
	abstract = {To address the complex operational characteristics, fluctuating efficiency, and insufficient predictive capability of new energy equipment, this study proposes a digital twin-based operational state prediction and optimization framework. The approach establishes a highfidelity virtual-physical mapping system integrating real-time sensor data, process models, and historical operational records. A hybrid prediction model combining Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCN) and gated recurrent units (GRU) is constructed to capture nonlinear temporal dependencies and improve fault trend prediction accuracy. The optimization layer introduces a multi-objective strategy based on Pareto dominance, balancing energy consumption, system stability, and prediction precision. The system architecture encompasses data acquisition, model training, and optimization control, achieving closed-loop synchronization between the physical and virtual environments. Experimental validation on a wind–solar hybrid energy platform demonstrates that the proposed model improves prediction accuracy by 9.4\%, enhances energy optimization efficiency by 12.7\%, and reduces latency by 31\% compared with conventional methods. The results confirm that digital twin-driven state prediction provides effective support for intelligent operation and maintenance, enabling adaptive scheduling and real-time fault prevention in new energy systems. This research offers practical guidance for constructing intelligent, efficient, and sustainable energy management frameworks in future industrial application.},
	language = {en},
