	url = {https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11490425/},
	doi = {10.1109/STPEC66316.2025.11490425},
	abstract = {The growing demand for high-performance electric vehicles (EVs) has intensified the need for intelligent battery thermal management systems (BTMS) to ensure safety, reliability, and energy efficiency. This study presents a novel digital twin (DT)driven framework for smart thermal regulation in lithium-ion battery packs, integrating real-time sensor data with a hybrid physicsinformed and data-driven model. The DT architecture synchronizes virtual battery simulations with real-world measurements, enabling predictive thermal control under transient operating conditions such as fast charging and aggressive driving. A reduced-order thermal model, calibrated against CFD simulations, is implemented within a MATLAB-Simulink environment to support rapid thermal forecasting and control decision-making. The system dynamically adapts cooling strategies including air, liquid, and Phase Changing Material (PCM)-assisted methods, based on predicted spatiotemporal heat fluxes, thereby enhancing thermal uniformity and reducing parasitic energy losses. Results demonstrate a 50\% reduction in cell-to-cell temperature gradients and 18–22\% savings in cooling energy consumption compared to conventional static BTMS. Furthermore, predictive control improves thermal response latency by up to 35\%, contributing to improved battery lifespan and overall energy efficiency. This work highlights the potential of DTenhanced BTMS as a scalable solution for next-generation EV platforms, bridging the gap between cyber-physical systems and real-time thermal optimization.},
	language = {en},
