	doi = {10.1109/ICPEE65973.2025.11411332},
	abstract = {The growing complications of grid-integrated photovoltaic (PV) systems and the necessity for continuous surveillance have promoted the creation of digital twins (DTs) as virtual representations of physical assets. Traditional physics-based DTs depend on intricate modeling and parameter identiﬁcation, which are computationally demanding and less ﬂexible in addressing unknown nonlinearities or component deterioration. This study introduces a data-driven digital twin framework for a single-stage, three-phase, four-leg grid-integrated photovoltaic inverter system, employing real-world data gathered from a hardware prototype under diverse operating conditions, including variations in irradiance and voltage sag. The DT model is implemented on an OPAL-RT OP4512 platform, while the experimental prototype is governed by a LabVIEW-based system on an NI sbRIO-9636 FPGA. A multi-output feedforward neural network (NN) is trained to precisely duplicate eight essential system signals, including grid current, DC-link voltage, inductor voltages, and ﬁlter capacitor dynamics. The proposed data-driven DT attains high replication ﬁdelity with minimum prediction error, establishing a basis for advanced applications including parameter degradation estimates, condition monitoring, and fault diagnostics.},
	language = {en},
