	doi = {10.1109/ICPEA67476.2025.11349303},
	abstract = {To address the technical bottlenecks in traditional Gas-Insulated Switchgear (GIS) fault diagnosis—such as overreliance on manual experience, shallow data fusion, and insufﬁcient fault localization—this paper proposes an intelligent diagnosis architecture based on multi-agent collaboration and digital twin. The architecture features a multi-agent system with ﬁve specialized units: data acquisition, digital twin modeling, multi-physics simulation, fault inversion diagnosis, and coordinated early warning. This paper systematically elaborates on the functional positioning, core technologies, and collaborative mechanisms of each agent. The proposed method constructs a high-ﬁdelity digital twin by integrating 3D laser point cloud data with design models. It deduces fault evolution mechanisms through multi-physics simulations and achieves precise traceability from macroscopic signals to microscopic fault sources using a deep learning-based inversion model. The results indicate that this architecture efﬁciently integrates multi-source heterogeneous information, enabling precise perception of GIS operational status, quantitative diagnosis of faults, and prediction of developmental trends. The multi-agent system signiﬁcantly enhances the automation and intelligence of GIS fault diagnosis, providing a new theoretical framework for achieving condition assessment and predictive maintenance of power equipment.},
