	doi = {10.1109/ICEACE67491.2025.11439327},
	abstract = {The present paper proposes a methodology for optimising the dynamic performance of electromechanical systems by integrating digital twins (DTs) and artificial intelligence (AI). To this end, a five-dimensional, high-order DT framework incorporating the physical, data, model, function, and application layers is constructed. The mechanism and AI surrogate models are then integrated to strike a balance between model accuracy and computational efficiency. Furthermore, a deep reinforcement learning (DRL) mechanism is introduced to train intelligent agents in the virtual twin environment to enable autonomous decision-making. Following safety verification, these agents are deployed to the physical system, forming a closed-loop optimisation process. The safety verification process uses a Monte Carlo simulation with 1,000 random samples, requiring the system's constraint satisfaction rate under the optimisation strategy to be ≥99\%, thereby ensuring the reliability of the optimisation decisions applied to the physical system. The experimental results demonstrate significant performance improvements. These include a 27.6\% reduction in contour error and a 7.9\% decrease in energy consumption for CNC spindle systems. There is also sustained tracking accuracy below 0.12 mm for industrial robot joints during long-term operation.},
	language = {en},
