	abstract = {The construcƟon sector is under growing pressure to reduce energy use and carbon emissions while sustaining operaƟonal eĸciency. This study develops and validates an integrated framework for real-Ɵme opƟmizaƟon of crane energy performance by combining digital twin technology with Lean ConstrucƟon principles. Three cranes—two tower cranes (C1, C2) and one mobile crane (MC) were monitored on a large construcƟon site in Billund, Denmark, using IoT-enabled sensors over seven weeks, generaƟng more than 16,000 ten-minute records. Key performance indicators (Idle Factor, Idle Time, and Night Baseload Index) were deĮned to capture non-producƟve energy use and linked to Lean concepts of waste eliminaƟon and conƟnuous improvement. Results revealed that idle periods dominated crane energy proĮles, with idle energy accounƟng for nearly 100\% of C1’s preparatory stage, 52\% of C2’s consumpƟon, and 42\% of MC’s. Aggregated idle emissions totaled 126 kgCO₂e, represenƟng approximately 55\% of total crane-related emissions. By embedding predicƟve analyƟcs and Lean-based KPIs into a digital twin plaƞorm, the framework delivers real-Ɵme visibility, automated alerts, and acƟonable insights for shutdown policies and task sequencing. This research provides one of the Įrst approaches explicitly connecƟng digital twins and Lean ConstrucƟon to reduce idle energy waste, advancing sustainable and data-driven site management.},
	language = {en},
