	doi = {10.1109/AIoT66900.2025.00109},
	abstract = {Assessing an athlete’s performance is critical in sports training, as it enables trainers to provide feedback to improve athletes’ skills and proficiency. While existing research focuses on digital twins to replicate an athlete’s physical states in cyberspace, these often lack the ability to assess and predict activities and performance. In this paper, we propose Digital PredTwin, a novel Artificial Intelligence of Things (AIoT) framework for constructing predictive digital twins of athletes, using full-body wearable Internet of Things (IoT) sensor data and Artificial Intelligence (AI) models. The framework assesses an athlete’s activities and performance using an ensemble machine learning (ML) model trained on real-world sports data across diverse participants, sports conditions, and genders. The resulting digital twins leverage trained AI models for activity and performance assessment and prediction. We validate the framework in a realworld sports environment, achieving an Area Under the Receiver Operating Characteristic Curve (AUC) of 0.84 for activity classification and 0.83 for performance assessment on the test dataset. This work highlights the potentials of the Digital PredTwin framework to accurately predict activity and assess performance in AIoT-enabled sports training.},
