	abstract = {In light of the rapid advancement of urbanization and increasing demand for subterranean space development, traditional underground construction faces challenges such as long project cycles, labor shortages, and high environmental risks. Prefabricated structures, widely applied in above-ground buildings for their efficiency, quality, and environmental benefits, are still in the early stage of adoption in underground engineering. To address the need for safety monitoring in complex underground environments, this study puts forward a digital twin. construction method for underground prefabricated structures based on steel sheet piles. The method integrates finite element analysis, proper orthogonal decomposition (POD), and machine learning techniques to establish a scaled cantilever steel plate model. By combining load identification with reduced-order modeling, it enables real-time prediction and visualization of structural stress and deformation. Using ANSYS Workbench and Twin Builder platforms, the study generates strain response matrices through APDL batch simulations, applies the influence coefficient method for load inversion, and builds static reduced-order models (ROM) in Twin Builder to achieve rapid digital twin computation. The system facilitates dynamic monitoring, load inversion, and safety warning during the construction process. Maintains high computational while significantly reducing computational cost and data latency, thereby providing a feasible pathway toward intelligent and resilient construction of underground prefabricated structures.},
