	doi = {10.1109/CSITSS67709.2025.11294106},
	abstract = {With the advent of 5G technology, wireless communication has entered a transformative phase, offering ultra-low latency, high bandwidth, and massive device connectivity. Among its key innovations is Device-to-Device (D2D) communication, which allows nearby devices to communicate directly without routing data through a central base station. This capability reduces network congestion, minimizes latency, and enhances communication efﬁciency, making it highly beneﬁcial for realtime applications such as high-deﬁnition video streaming, lowdelay voice calling, and immersive online gaming. However, the growing number of connected devices and the varying demands of different applications create signiﬁcant challenges in managing scarce resources like bandwidth and transmission power. Traditional static or reactive resource allocation strategies often fall short in adapting to rapidly changing network conditions, resulting in inefﬁcient spectrum utilization, increased interference, and degraded Quality of Service (QoS).},
