	abstract = {With the advancement of digital twins for real-time predictive analytics, there is a growing demand for continual learning systems that can adapt dynamically and be retrained efﬁciently. In this context, we focus on a deep learning approach using a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model to predict Remaining Useful Life (RUL). RUL, deﬁned as the time or operational cycles remaining before a system fails, is central to predictive maintenance and digital twin applications, enabling proactive scheduling of repairs, minimizing downtime, and improving system reliability. However, as these real-time models are retrained, performance degrades due to an imbalance of prioritizing new, incoming data over previously learned knowledge. This presents a challenge in digital twin environments, where historical data often remains relevant for predicting future outcomes. Therefore, it is critical to adopt retraining strategies that are both efﬁcient and mitigate forgetting.},
